1.  WHAT THIS TOOL DOES

This tool takes TWO CSV files — each representing a different population of
objects (e.g. cells, puncta, organelles, or molecular markers from two
fluorescent channels) — and performs a comprehensive colocalization analysis.

It answers questions like:
  • Are these two populations spatially associated (clustered together)?
  • Are they randomly distributed relative to each other?
  • Are they actively avoiding each other (segregated)?
  • How much do their spatial distributions overlap?
  • Which objects from population A are near objects from population B?

The tool produces 9 publication-quality figures and a summary CSV of all
computed metrics. Every run is timestamped so you never lose previous results.
2.  INSTALLATION REQUIREMENTS
You need the following installed on your computer:
  A) Python (version 3.8 or newer)
     Download from: https://www.python.org/downloads/
          IMPORTANT for Windows users:
       During installation, CHECK the box that says:
       ☑ "Add Python to PATH"
  B) Required Python packages
     The tool uses these libraries (all free and open-source):
     Package           What it does                    
     numpy             Numerical computations           
     pandas            Reading CSV files                 
     matplotlib        Creating figures                  
     scipy             Spatial statistics, KDE, Voronoi  
     scikit-learn      K-means, DBSCAN clustering        
     networkx          Network/graph analysis            
     tkinter           GUI (usually included with Python) 
  C) Operating System
     Works on Windows, macOS, and Linux.
     The GUI uses tkinter, which comes pre-installed with Python on Windows
     and macOS. On Linux you may need to install it separately (see below).

3.  STEP-BY-STEP INSTALLATION
  STEP 1:  Install Python
    • Go to https://www.python.org/downloads/
    • Download the latest Python 3.x release
    • Run the installer
    • Windows: CHECK "Add Python to PATH" at the bottom of the installer
    • macOS: Just follow the prompts
    • Click "Install Now"
  STEP 2:  Open a terminal / command prompt
      • Windows: Press Win+R, type "cmd", press Enter
               OR search for "Command Prompt" in the Start menu
               OR search for "PowerShell"
    • macOS:   Open Applications → Utilities → Terminal
    • Linux:   Open your terminal application
  STEP 3:  Install required packages
    Copy and paste this single command into your terminal and press Enter:
    pip install numpy pandas matplotlib scipy scikit-learn networkx
    Wait for it to finish. You should see "Successfully installed ..."
    If "pip" is not found, try:
        python -m pip install numpy pandas matplotlib scipy scikit-learn networkx
    On macOS/Linux, you might need:
        pip3 install numpy pandas matplotlib scipy scikit-learn networkx
  STEP 4 (Linux only):  Install tkinter
      On Ubuntu/Debian:
      sudo apt-get install python3-tk
    On Fedora:
      sudo dnf install python3-tkinter
    On macOS and Windows, tkinter is included with Python automatically.
  STEP 5:  Save the script
    Save the Python script as "colocalisation.py" in a folder you can
    easily find (e.g. your Desktop or a project folder).
  STEP 6:  Test the installation
    In your terminal, navigate to the folder where you saved the script:
    
     cd path/to/your/folder
    Then run:
          python colocalisation.py
    The GUI window should appear. If it does, everything is installed
    correctly!
4.  PREPARING YOUR DATA (CSV Format)
  WHAT YOU NEED
  Two CSV files, one for each population/channel you want to compare.
  For example:
    • "GFP_puncta.csv"    — objects detected in the green channel
    • "mCherry_puncta.csv" — objects detected in the red channel
  REQUIRED COLUMNS
  Each CSV file MUST contain at least these columns (header names must
  match exactly, including underscores and lowercase):
    Column Name      What It Represents                   Units
    centroid_x       X-coordinate of the object centre    pixels or µm
    centroid_y       Y-coordinate of the object centre    pixels or µm
    area             Size/area of the object              pixels² or µm²
  OPTIONAL COLUMNS (ignored but allowed)
  The tool will silently ignore any extra columns. So if your CSV also has
  columns like "label", "solidity", "n_cells_estimate", etc., that is fine.
  EXAMPLE CSV FILE
  Your file might look like this when opened in a text editor:
    label,area,centroid_y,centroid_x,solidity,n_cells_estimate
    1,245.5,100.2,200.3,0.95,1
    2,189.3,150.7,180.4,0.88,1
    3,312.1,220.1,340.2,0.92,2
    4,156.8,310.5,120.9,0.91,1

  Or with tab separation:

    label	area	centroid_y	centroid_x	solidity	n_cells_estimate
    1	245.5	100.2	200.3	0.95	1
    2	189.3	150.7	180.4	0.88	1

  Both comma-separated and tab-separated files are supported. The tool
  auto-detects the delimiter.
  IMPORTANT NOTES
    • Both CSV files should use the SAME coordinate system and units.
    If one is in pixels and the other in micrometres, the results will
    be meaningless.
  • If your data comes from different images, make sure the coordinates
    are registered (aligned) to the same reference frame.
  • Common sources that produce compatible CSVs:
      - StarDist / Cellpose (cell segmentation)
      - ImageJ/FIJI (Analyze Particles)
      - QuPath
      - CellProfiler
      - Custom Python scripts using scikit-image regionprops
  • The more objects you have, the more statistically robust the analysis
    will be. The tool works with as few as ~10 objects per file, but
    ideally you want 50+ in each.
5.  USING THE GUI (Graphical Interface)
  LAUNCHING THE GUI
    Double-click the script file, or open a terminal and run:

    python colocalisation.py
  STEP-BY-STEP
  ────────────
  1. Click "Browse…" next to CSV File A → select your first CSV file
     (e.g. GFP channel detections)

  2. Click "Browse…" next to CSV File B → select your second CSV file
     (e.g. mCherry channel detections)

  3. (Optional) Change the population labels.
     These names appear on all the figures and in the summary.
     Examples: "GFP", "mCherry", "Marker X", "CD4+ T cells", etc.

  4. The output directory defaults to the same folder as CSV File A.
     If you want results saved elsewhere:
       - Uncheck "Same directory as CSV A"
       - Click "Browse…" to pick a different folder

  5. Click "▶ Run Analysis"

  6. Wait for the progress bar and status message.
     The analysis typically takes 10 seconds to a few minutes depending
     on how many objects you have.

  7. When finished, a popup will confirm success and show you where
     the results were saved.

  8. Navigate to the output folder to find your figures and summary CSV.

  NOTES
  ─────
  • You can run the analysis multiple times with different files or
    parameters — each run gets a unique timestamp (e.g. 20250125_143052)
    so nothing is overwritten.

  • The GUI stays responsive during analysis (it runs in the background).
    Do not close the window while it is running.

  • If something goes wrong, an error dialog will appear with details
    about what went wrong.

7.  UNDERSTANDING THE OUTPUT FILES
  After a successful run, you will find these files in your output folder
  (all with a timestamp suffix like _20250125_143052):

  File                           			    What It Shows
  01_spatial_overlay_*.png           Side-by-side and merged scatter plots
                                     of both populations. Marker size
                                     reflects object area.

  02_nearest_neighbour_*.png         How far each object in A is from its
                                     closest neighbour in B (and vice
                                     versa). Includes histograms, CDFs,
                                     and a spatial map with connecting
                                     lines.

  03_grid_correlation_*.png          The image is divided into a grid;
                                     counts of A and B per grid square
                                     are compared. Shows density maps,
                                     merged image, and correlation plot
                                     with Pearson/Spearman/Manders.

  04_proximity_network_*.png         Network where A and B objects are
                                     connected if within a threshold
                                     distance. Shown at multiple
                                     thresholds. Nodes coloured by degree
                                     (number of cross-type connections).

  04b_degree_distribution_*.png      Histograms of how many cross-type
                                     neighbours each object has, and
                                     whether larger objects have more.

  05_kmeans_*.png                    K-means clustering of all objects
                                     together. Shows elbow plot,
                                     silhouette, mixing entropy, and
                                     spatial cluster map.

  05b_kmeans_composition_*.png       Stacked bar chart: what fraction of
                                     each cluster is A vs B.

  06_dbscan_*.png                    Density-based clustering (DBSCAN).
                                     Highlights "mixed" clusters that
                                     contain both A and B objects, with
                                     convex hull outlines.

  07_ripleys_cross_K_*.png           Ripley's cross-K and cross-L spatial
                                     statistics with simulation envelope.
                                     Tests whether colocalization is
                                     statistically significant.

  08_kde_overlap_*.png               Smooth density surfaces (KDE) for
                                     each population and their overlap
                                     visualized as an RGB merge.

  09_voronoi_*.png                   Voronoi tessellation showing which
                                     objects neighbour objects of the
                                     other type. Measures spatial mixing
                                     vs segregation.

  summary_metrics_*.csv              All numerical metrics from every
                                     analysis in one spreadsheet. Open
                                     in Excel, Google Sheets, etc.
8.  TROUBLESHOOTING / FAQ
  Q: "python" is not recognised as a command.
  A: Python is not in your PATH. On Windows, reinstall Python and check
     "Add Python to PATH". Or try "python3" instead of "python".

  Q: "pip" is not recognised.
  A: Try: python -m pip install ...
     Or:  python3 -m pip install ...

  Q: I get "ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tkinter'"
  A: On Linux: sudo apt-get install python3-tk
     On macOS/Windows this should not happen — reinstall Python.

  Q: I get "Missing columns in file: {'centroid_x'}"
  A: Your CSV does not have the required column names. Check that the
     header row contains exactly: centroid_x, centroid_y, area
     (case-sensitive, with underscores).

  Q: The analysis is very slow.
  A: With very large datasets (>10,000 objects), some analyses (especially
     Ripley's K and proximity networks) can take several minutes. This is
     normal. The progress bar will keep spinning.

  Q: Can I compare more than two populations?
  A: Run the tool multiple times with different pairs. For example:
     Run 1: Channel A vs Channel B
     Run 2: Channel A vs Channel C
     Run 3: Channel B vs Channel C
     Each run is timestamped separately.

  Q: What coordinate units should I use?
  A: Any consistent units work (pixels, micrometres, etc.). Just make
     sure BOTH files use the same units. The scale bar on plots will
     show "units" — you can mentally substitute your actual unit.

  Q: My CSVs use different delimiters (comma vs tab).
  A: The tool auto-detects the delimiter. Both comma and tab separation
     work fine.

  Q: Can I use this with 3D data (X, Y, Z)?
  A: Currently the tool analyses 2D colocalization only (X, Y). If you
     have 3D data, you could run it on 2D projections, or modify the
     script to include a Z coordinate.

  Q: I ran the analysis twice and got different files — is that right?
  A: Yes! Each run is timestamped (e.g. _20250125_143052 vs
     _20250125_143510). Nothing is ever overwritten. You can safely
     run the tool as many times as you like.

